            <h2>Step-by-Step Journey Experience</h2>
            <p>Understanding the full journey from Baltimore to Boston can make your travel experience smoother and more predictable. Here’s what to expect from station arrival to reaching your destination.</p>
            <h2>Arrival at the Station in Baltimore</h2>
            <p>Most trains to Boston depart from Baltimore Penn Station, conveniently located near downtown Baltimore.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Recommended Arrival: </b>20-30 minutes before departure.</li>
                <li><b>Facilities:</b> Ticket counters, waiting areas, restrooms, small cafés, and parking.</li>
                <li><b>Signage: </b>Platforms and departure boards are clearly marked for easy navigation.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Boarding Process</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Platforms are usually announced 15-20 minutes before departure.</li>
                <li>Boarding is straightforward, with clearly marked coach and business class cars.</li>
                <li>No airport-style security checks, making the process quick and simple.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Onboard Experience</h3>
            <p>The Baltimore → Boston train journey is designed for comfort and relaxation:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Seating:</b> Spacious, adjustable, with good legroom.</li>
                <li><b>Amenities:</b> Overhead storage, power outlets, and free Wi‑Fi (where available).</li>
                <li><b>Environment:</b> Quiet, especially in Business Class and Acela.</li>
                <li><b>Scenery:</b> Enjoy views of the Chesapeake Bay, Delaware River, Philadelphia skyline, New York City, Connecticut shoreline, and Massachusetts countryside.</li>
                <li><b>Café Car: </b>Offers snacks, beverages, and light meals during the trip.</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Arrival in Boston</h2>
            <p>Trains from Baltimore arrive at Boston South Station, located in downtown Boston, making it easy to start exploring immediately upon arrival.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Location:</b> City center, near major streets and transit hubs.</li>
                <li><b>Connectivity:</b> Close to MBTA Red Line, Silver Line (to Logan Airport), buses, taxis, and rideshare pick-up zones.</li>
                <li><b>Facilities:</b> Waiting areas, ticket counters, retail shops, cafés, restrooms, and luggage storage.</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Tips to Save Money on This Route</h2>
            <p>Managing the cost of train travel from Baltimore to Boston is easier with some planning and smart strategies. Prices can vary depending on timing, class, and travel season, but with the right approach, you can save money without compromising comfort.</p>
            <h3>Plan for Better Price Options</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Advance Booking: </b>Purchasing tickets days or weeks ahead often secures the lowest fares, especially for Northeast Regional Coach seating.</li>
                <li><b>Compare Departures:</b> Check multiple departure times to find the most economical option.</li>
                <li><b>Seating Choices: </b>Coach is generally cheaper than Business Class; consider if comfort trade-offs are acceptable.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Travel During Off-Peak Hours</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Midday Trains: </b>Often less crowded, with fares sometimes lower than early morning or evening trains.</li>
                <li><b>Avoid Peak Travel:</b> Weekends, holidays, and popular times often lead to higher prices and limited availability.</li>
                <li><b>Flexibility Helps: </b>Traveling on weekdays can reduce cost and provide a more relaxed environment.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Stay Flexible with Your Travel Plans</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Adjusting your departure by a few hours or considering different travel days can unlock cheaper options.</li>
                <li>Flexibility may also improve seating availability, especially in Business Class.</li>
                <li>Being open to slightly longer travel times or non-prime hours can balance cost and convenience.</li>
            </ul>
